}


---

22. BASELINE REQUIREMENT

No meaningful candidate should be evaluated in isolation.

3F requires an appropriate baseline.

Examples:

buy-and-hold
simple rule
randomized baseline
previous champion
naive forecast
existing strategy

The correct baseline depends on the research question.

The question is:

> Did the candidate actually add value?




---

23. BACKTEST VERIFICATION

Backtesting is evidence.

It is not proof.

3F checks:

data integrity
look-ahead bias
survivorship bias
selection bias
corporate actions
delistings
missing data
timestamp correctness
execution assumptions
transaction costs
slippage
liquidity
position constraints
capacity
out-of-sample performance
walk-forward behavior
regime behavior
drawdowns
failure conditions

A backtest that ignores realistic execution conditions is not equivalent to live evidence.


---

24. FORWARD / WALK-FORWARD TESTING

3F treats forward testing separately from ordinary historical backtesting.

Concept:

TRAIN
  ↓
TEST ON UNSEEN PERIOD
  ↓
MOVE WINDOW
  ↓
RETRAIN / REFIT
  ↓
TEST NEXT UNSEEN PERIOD
  ↓
REPEAT

This tests whether the strategy survives changing information conditions.

It is especially useful for adaptive systems.


---

25. REPLAY VERIFICATION

Replay reconstructs a historical event stream as if CNS were operating at that point in time.

Critical rule:

> CNS must only use information that would have been available at the simulated decision time.



Therefore:

Historical timestamp
      ↓
Available data
      ↓
Available memory snapshot
      ↓
Available model version
      ↓
Available policy version
      ↓
Decision
      ↓
Outcome

Future information must remain inaccessible.

This connects directly to 3E's temporal memory architecture.


---

26. SIMULATION

Simulation explores possible futures or controlled synthetic environments.

Examples:

market stress
liquidity shock
volatility expansion
gap
slippage increase
execution failure
provider outage
model failure
correlated positions
extreme drawdown

Simulation answers:

> “What could happen under controlled conditions?”



It does not prove that reality will follow the simulation.


---

27. PAPER TRADING

Paper trading is an intermediate evidence layer.

It tests:

real-time data
real-time timing
signal generation
decision latency
execution logic
order assumptions
state transitions
operational stability
strategy behavior

But paper trading still differs from real capital because:

market impact
slippage
psychological pressure
actual liquidity
real execution
capital constraints

may differ.

Therefore:

BACKTEST
≠
FORWARD TEST
≠
REPLAY
≠
SIMULATION
≠
PAPER
≠
LIVE

3F keeps these evidence types distinct.


---

28. STATISTICAL DISCIPLINE

3F explicitly checks:

multiple testing
data snooping
overfitting
selection bias
survivorship bias
look-ahead bias
parameter mining
regime selection
p-hacking
false discovery
small sample problems
non-stationarity

No single statistical metric determines acceptance.


---

29. CALIBRATION

When CNS predicts probability, 3F evaluates whether those probabilities deserve trust.

Brier score:

\[
Brier =
\frac{1}{N}
\sum_{i=1}^{N}(p_i-o_i)^2
\]

where:

p_i = predicted probability
o_i = actual outcome

Calibration must be evaluated across:

time
market regime
strategy
model
confidence range
resource
task type

A model that says “90%” and succeeds 55% of the time is not a 90% model.


---

30. ROBUSTNESS

A result is stronger when it survives reasonable changes.

3F tests:

parameter perturbation
time-window changes
cost assumptions
slippage
execution assumptions
market regimes
data variations
feature removal
model variations
random seeds
sample splits
stress scenarios

The goal is not:

> “Make the backtest survive everything.”



The goal is:

> Understand where the result survives and where it fails.




---

31. ABLATION

Ablation asks:

> “Which component actually creates the result?”



Example:

Full system
   ↓
remove feature A
   ↓
measure

remove feature B
   ↓
measure

remove model X
   ↓
measure

This prevents CNS from carrying unnecessary complexity.

It directly supports the Log 2 principle:

> complexity must earn its place.




---

32. CHAMPION VS CANDIDATE

Every production-worthy research component can have:

CHAMPION
CANDIDATE

Candidate must beat or materially improve the champion under predefined criteria.

Then:

Candidate
 ↓
Shadow
 ↓
Canary
 ↓
Promotion

or:

Candidate
 ↓
Reject
 ↓
Keep evidence

This connects to controlled self-improvement.


---

33. REPRODUCIBILITY

Every significant experiment must preserve enough information to reproduce it.

At minimum:

data version
code version
model version
configuration
environment
random seed where applicable
baseline
metrics
cost assumptions
test periods
results
limitations

A result that cannot be reconstructed should have reduced evidentiary status.


---

34. OPPORTUNITY RESEARCH PRIORITY

Not every interesting possibility deserves equal research.

Research priority can consider:

potential upside
asymmetry
probability
uncertainty
consequence
time sensitivity
VOI
research cost
exploitability
competitive advantage

Conceptually:

\[
ResearchPriority
\sim
\frac{PotentialValue \times VOI \times TimeSensitivity}
{ResearchCost + CoordinationCost}
\]

This is an architectural heuristic, not a sacred formula.


---

35. NOVEL OPPORTUNITY PROTOCOL

This is extremely important for CNS.

Suppose CNS detects:

> something unusual that has little historical precedent.



A conservative system might say:

No historical proof → reject.

CNS should instead do:

Novel signal
 ↓
Reality check
 ↓
Mechanism check
 ↓
Evidence search
 ↓
Contradiction search
 ↓
Asymmetry analysis
 ↓
Can it be tested cheaply?
       ↓
YES → experiment
NO  → deeper research
 ↓
EARLY_OPPORTUNITY
 ↓
paper / simulation / small controlled test
 ↓
learn

This allows CNS to hunt for things conventional systems may miss.


---

36. MINIMUM SUFFICIENT VERIFICATION

3F must continuously ask:

> What is the minimum evidence required for this particular decision?



Not:

> “What is the maximum amount of research we can perform?”



This is fundamental to CNS speed.


---

37. STOPPING RULE

Research must have stopping conditions.

Possible stopping reasons:

Question resolved
Evidence overwhelmingly supports
Evidence overwhelmingly contradicts
VOI too low
Research cost too high
Time sensitivity exceeded
Opportunity disappeared
Required data unavailable
Contradiction cannot be resolved
Risk exceeds authority

No endless research loop.


---

38. DECISION EVALUATION

3F compares available choices.

Not only:

ACT

but:

ACT
WAIT
RESEARCH
SIMULATE
PAPER
SMALL TEST
MONITOR
REJECT

For each:

expected outcomes
probabilities
upside
downside
uncertainty
cost
time
reversibility
risk
information gained

This is much more intelligent than binary approval/rejection.


---

39. ALTERNATIVE ANALYSIS

Every important decision should ask:

> What if the best action is not the obvious action?



For example:

A = invest now
B = wait
C = investigate
D = paper trade
E = invest smaller
F = choose another opportunity

CNS should compare alternatives rather than verify one preferred answer.

This reduces confirmation bias.


---

40. DISSENT / MINORITY EVIDENCE

If most resources say:

YES

but one credible independent resource says:

NO

3F must preserve the dissent.

It must not simply average the opinions.

The minority evidence becomes:

investigation target

especially if the dissent concerns:

fraud
liquidity
risk
model failure
data integrity
structural change


---

41. VERIFICATION INDEPENDENCE

3F must avoid:

same model
same data
same reasoning chain
same assumptions

being presented as independent confirmation.

For important decisions, independent verification should differ in at least some relevant dimension:

resource
method
data source
model
reasoning path
assumption set


---

42. DECISION CONSEQUENCE MATRIX

Conceptual matrix:

Uncertainty	Consequence	3F response

Low	Low	Fast check
Low	High	Strong verification
High	Low	Cheap experiment
High	High	Deep investigation
Novel	Low	Explore
Novel	High	Research + controlled test
Strong evidence	Favorable asymmetry	Pursue within authority
Strong evidence against	Any	Reject rapidly


This is the anti-paralysis architecture.


---

43. POST-DECISION EVALUATION

3F does not disappear after CNS decides.

It records:

What did we believe?
Why?
Probability?
Expected outcome?
Expected downside?
Expected upside?
What actually happened?

Then:

EXPECTED
vs
ACTUAL

Difference becomes error information.


---

44. ERROR ATTRIBUTION

When a prediction fails, 3F should not simply say:

> “Model was wrong.”



Possible causes:

bad data
bad feature
bad assumption
wrong regime
model error
calibration error
execution error
research error
verification error
orchestration error
memory error
unexpected event
structural change

This is crucial.

Otherwise CNS could “learn” the wrong lesson.


---

45. 3F → 3E LEARNING LOOP

Research
 ↓
Decision
 ↓
Outcome
 ↓
Expected vs Actual
 ↓
Error attribution
 ↓
Lesson candidate
 ↓
3E validation
 ↓
Experience
 ↓
Knowledge

3F generates evidence about what happened.

3E owns durable experience.


---

46. 3F → 3B

3B generates:

hypotheses
questions
plans
predictions

3F returns:

verified evidence
contradictions
uncertainty
calibration
research results
decision readiness

Therefore:

3B → 3F
3F → 3B

This creates an iterative cognitive loop.


---

47. 3F → 3C

3F may determine:

Need financial research
Need competitor analysis
Need historical replay
Need independent valuation
Need fraud check
Need stress test

But 3F does not create/manage the work graph itself.

It sends the requirement to 3C.

3F:
"Research requirement identified."

3C:
"How should this work be decomposed and assigned?"

3D:
"What resources can execute it?"

3F:
"Are the results sufficient?"

Clean boundary.


---

48. 3F → 3D

3F may require:

statistical engine
backtest engine
ML model
LLM
data provider
verification tool
simulation engine
human

3D supplies qualified resources.

3F does not own their lifecycle.


---

49. 3F → 3E

3F writes only appropriate knowledge through the memory authority established in 3E.

Important information:

research result
verified claim
failed hypothesis
calibration evidence
decision outcome
failure mode
historical precedent
contradiction
research limitation

Temporal snapshots remain mandatory.


---

50. RESEARCH LEDGER

Every significant research decision produces a durable ledger entry.

decision_id
date
problem
opportunity
capability
hypothesis
mechanism
candidates
baseline
experiment
data_version
model_version
configuration
metrics
failures
contradictions
statistical_tests
robustness
security
cost
latency
result
decision
reason
limitations
next_experiment
provenance

This becomes part of CNS's institutional memory.


---

51. RESEARCH SECURITY

Research systems can create serious vulnerabilities.

3F must protect against:

data poisoning
future-data leakage
tampered datasets
fake evidence
malicious tools
result manipulation
experiment configuration tampering
credential leakage
untrusted code
unsafe external execution

Research output cannot automatically gain execution authority.


---

52. DATA CONTAMINATION DEFENSE

Especially important for financial research.

3F must distinguish:

historical information available at T

from:

information learned after T

For historical simulation:

KnowledgeSnapshot
+
data version
+
memory cutoff
+
model version
+
policy version

must define the information boundary.

This connects directly to 3E.


---

53. FAILURE STATES

3F must explicitly represent:

INSUFFICIENT_DATA
CONTRADICTORY_EVIDENCE
STALE_EVIDENCE
LOW_SOURCE_RELIABILITY
RESEARCH_TIMEOUT
NON_REPRODUCIBLE
STATISTICAL_WEAKNESS
ROBUSTNESS_FAILURE
ADVERSARIAL_FAILURE
DATA_CONTAMINATION
LOOKAHEAD_DETECTED
EXECUTION_ASSUMPTION_INVALID
MODEL_DRIFT
REGIME_BREAK
UNKNOWN_FAILURE

Failure is information.


---

54. FAILURE RECOVERY

When verification fails:

Classify failure
 ↓
Preserve evidence
 ↓
Determine blast radius
 ↓
Invalidate affected conclusion
 ↓
Create research requirement
 ↓
Re-run only necessary work
 ↓
Re-evaluate

Do not silently continue using contaminated results.


---

55. RESEARCH REUSE

If CNS already performed valid research:

reuse result

when:

same question
same relevant data
same temporal validity
same assumptions
same model/configuration
result still fresh
no invalidating event

Otherwise:

revalidate

This prevents repeated expensive research.


---

56. COGNITIVE BRANCHING

3F may support multiple competing hypotheses:

H1
H2
H3
H4

instead of forcing early commitment.

Each receives:

evidence
contradictions
probability
failure conditions
VOI
research requirements

CNS can later eliminate branches.

This is superior to premature certainty.


---

57. HIGH-CONVICTION READY

This state is reserved for unusually strong cases.

Requirements may include:

strong evidence
independent confirmation
good provenance
low unresolved contradiction
good calibration
robustness
appropriate historical/OOS evidence
favorable asymmetry
acceptable downside
fresh information
clear invalidation conditions
execution feasibility

But:

> HIGH_CONVICTION_READY never bypasses risk, authority or CNS decision control.




---

58. CRITICAL TRADING BOUNDARY

3F must never become a single point of failure for live trading.

Live hot path remains:

MARKET DATA
 ↓
FEATURES
 ↓
STRATEGY
 ↓
RISK
 ↓
ORDER VALIDATION
 ↓
EXECUTION

3F can:

research strategy
verify strategy
evaluate regime
detect degradation
recommend suspension
evaluate changes

But the live deterministic safety architecture remains independent.


---

59. HUMAN ESCALATION

Some situations require human authority.

3F may return:

HUMAN_REVIEW_REQUIRED

when:

authority insufficient
consequence extreme
evidence irreconcilable
novel risk
legal/compliance boundary
capital threshold
irreversible action
system uncertainty extreme

This is not failure.

It is controlled escalation.


---

60. CORE INTERFACES

Verification

trait VerificationEngine {
    define_claim(...)
    collect_evidence(...)
    verify(...)
    challenge(...)
    detect_contradictions(...)
    assess_uncertainty(...)
    assess_robustness(...)
    produce_result(...)
}

Research

trait ResearchEngine {
    define_experiment(...)
    run(...)
    validate(...)
    compare_baseline(...)
    run_oos(...)
    run_walk_forward(...)
    run_stress(...)
    run_ablation(...)
    evaluate(...)
}

Decision evaluation

trait DecisionEvaluator {
    evaluate_options(...)
    estimate_outcomes(...)
    assess_asymmetry(...)
    calculate_voi(...)
    assess_readiness(...)
    identify_missing_evidence(...)
}

Reproducibility

trait ResearchLedger {
    record(...)
    retrieve(...)
    reproduce(...)
    compare(...)
}


